  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over the square x, z from −2 to 2. It is 3 times steeper along x, so a learning rate above 2/3 overshoots more each step along x and diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has standard deviation 2 × √((64−B)/(63B)) per direction for batch size B. That is 2 at B=1 and exactly 0 at B=64.",
    "Both arrows use the same scale, 1 scene unit per unit of step: orange is the actual next step (mini-batch), grey is −learning rate × true slope. The step length readout is the orange arrow's length.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn square. A diverged ball is drawn at the bowl's rim, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and continues in sequence across new runs. Reset replays it from the start. The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 steps (41 points). The ghost holds the same."
  ]
}